+\begin{statement}{B.24}{Refuted}{The unrestricted minimal integral comparison fails}
+The fine signed conjugator datum in \S51.2, C4 scan 41, does not
+in general lift from its minimal rank-two model to the canonical
+integral abelian-kernel stage of the $q_2$ Fermat covering, compatibly
+with the modular extension.  The failure occurs over the smallest
+dual-number ring $A=\F_2[\epsilon]/\epsilon^2$, even with both
+conjugator signs positive and multiplier one.
+
+More precisely, with the integral models and canonical projection
+$\pi:\mathscr C_2\to\mathscr C_0$ constructed in B.24a, the valid
+datum
+\[
+ \alpha_0=\beta_0=\epsilon x,\qquad
+ \nu=\nu'=\nu_2=\nu_3=0,\qquad\mu=1
+\]
+defines $u=\operatorname{Int}(\epsilon x)$ on $\mathscr C_{0,A}$.
+There is no group-scheme automorphism $\Phi$ of $\mathscr C_{2,A}$
+with $\pi\Phi=u\pi$, even after a faithfully flat extension of $A$.
+Thus no unrestricted integral functor comparison with that compatibility
+can be recovered simply by retaining the minimal conjugators and signs.
+\end{statement}
+\begin{proof}
+Conjugation on $M=\Z x\oplus\Z y$ has the matrices $R,S$ in B.24b.
+They give
+$(1-R)(\epsilon,0)^t=(1-S)(\epsilon,0)^t=(\epsilon,\epsilon)^t$.
+Hence the scan-41 equations hold with exactly the stated data.
+This is an actual inner automorphism.  It preserves the loop subgroup,
+because $x=\omega(\sigma\rho)^2$ commutes with $\sigma\rho$.
+It sends $\rho$ to $(\epsilon,\epsilon)\rho$.
